<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements for the TrollStore build of the .NET harness.

  These are exactly the entitlements TrollStore grants an app it installs, minus
  get-task-allow, with the container identifier pointing at THIS app's bundle id
  rather than the Flutter client's. The Flutter session read the original set off
  the device rather than guessing, and this file reproduces it.

  Why omit get-task-allow. It is iOS's debuggable flag, scored +35 by
  ios_get_task_allow, and TrollStore sets it on everything it installs so its JIT
  option works. That gave the test iPhone a permanent floor: the device could
  never read trusted, so no clean iOS baseline was obtainable. Suppressing it
  server-side with INTEGRITY_ALLOW_DEBUG is the wrong answer, because that flag
  gates six rules across both platforms including ios_process_traced (+50), and
  would blind the server to a real debugger attaching.

  Why keep keychain-access-groups byte for byte. The Secure Enclave key AND the
  installation state both live in that access group. Change the value and both
  become unreachable, the app silently mints a new key and a new installation id,
  and the identity resets on every rebuild, which would make battery item 15
  fail for a reason that has nothing to do with what item 15 tests.

  Applied by tools/presign_trollstore_ipa.sh, which fails closed if
  get-task-allow survives signing.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>application-identifier</key>
	<string>TROLLTROLL.*</string>
	<key>com.apple.developer.team-identifier</key>
	<string>TROLLTROLL</string>
	<key>com.apple.private.security.container-required</key>
	<string>com.example.devicefingerprinting_dotnet</string>
	<key>keychain-access-groups</key>
	<array>
		<string>TROLLTROLL.*</string>
		<string>com.apple.token</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
